a controversial new proposal aims to  stop overcrowding in one of the world's  most popular tourist destinations about  30 million visitors pack into the  Italian city of Venice every year  it is a beautiful city to deal with the  crowds the city is planning to start  charging many tourists an entrance fee  Seth Doane is in Venice where locals say  the city's future is at stake Seth good  morning good morning to you locals say  this place has become so busy it is like  a Disneyland for tourists this view has  always been seen as priceless but now it  in fact may come with a price upwards of  10 euros that's the u.s. equivalent of  about $11.50 per visits its famed canals  and picture-perfect setting has made  Venice infamous for its tourists hordes  pack its narrow alleys searching for  that all-important selfie but the vast  majority about four-fifths of visitors  do not spend the night so the city loses  out on potential earnings from hotel  taxes now Italy's parliament has  approved a measure that will allow the  city to charge tourists that only come  for the day could outfit but crystal  show the money would go towards keeping  the city clean Mayor Luigi Bruni o said  and allow locals to live with more  decorum Venetians have told us their  concerns the daily deluge of visitors  some coming on giant cruise ships is not  only straining the city's resources and  delicate architecture but causing Venice  to lose its soul since 1951 the city's  population has plummeted to fewer than  55 thousand people but it can see on  average more than 80 thousand visitors  per day we reported on the regular  protests from residents who have carried  suitcases as a symbol they're on their  way out we have many many too much  duties too many tourists yes  that's according to Palomar who manages  tourism for the city before the new tax  was announced she told us visitors are  essential to the economy but that the  city has reached a breaking point  tourists are our guests mark told us we  want to treat them with respect but we  want respect this is a fragile city  officials have put up gates so they can  control the flow and have proposed  raising a fine to more than $500 for  those sitting or lying on undesignated  public spaces  they've also banned the opening of new  fast-food chains out of concern Venice  was at risk of losing its identity the  tax will be higher during peak periods  that is not yet clear when or how this  fee will be implemented though it is  certain that other cities facing this  tourists crush will be watching Venice  closely Anthony it's worth it Seth still  my favorite city in the world was my  mom's favorite city in the world  unbelievable you're saying it could be  up to like 10 euros it's not all things  considered it's not a lot they should  tax those mammoths ships that come in  with those big wakes yes Seth Doane  thanks
 

